    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p130-task-01-05-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "The graph of an even function has reflective symmetry.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. An even function satisfies $f(-x)=f(x)$, so its graph has reflective symmetry about the $y$-axis.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p130-task-01-05-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "The graph of an odd function has rotational symmetry.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. An odd function satisfies $f(-x)=-f(x)$, so its graph has $180^\\circ$ rotational symmetry about the origin.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p130-task-01-05-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "The graph of all odd functions must pass through the origin.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ is odd but does not pass through the origin.",
          "worked": "False. Consider $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$, with $x\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=\\frac{1}{-x}\\\\f(-x)&=-\\frac{1}{x}\\\\f(-x)&=-f(x).\\end{align*}$$\nThus $f$ is odd, but $f(0)$ is undefined, so its graph does not pass through the origin. An odd function must satisfy $f(0)=0$ only when $0$ belongs to its domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p130-task-01-05-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "If $f(x)$ is odd, then $|f(x)|$ is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Since $f$ is odd, $f(-x)=-f(x)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}|f(-x)|&=|-f(x)|\\\\|f(-x)|&=|f(x)|.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $|f(x)|$ is even.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p130-task-01-05-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "If $f(x)$ is even, then $|f(x)|$ is odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. For $f(x)=x^2$, $|f(x)|=x^2$ is not odd.",
          "worked": "False. The function $f(x)=x^2$ is even and $|f(x)|=x^2$. However, $|f(-1)|=1$ while $-|f(1)|=-1$, so $|f(x)|$ does not satisfy the odd-function identity. In general, taking the absolute value of an even function preserves evenness.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
